+Chapter~\ref{ch:database} presents the Sensory Afferent Database, the structured afferent resource built to answer the afferent-literature gap. It describes the audited corpus and its curation coverage, in which papers carry structured curation notes, afferent classifications, and notes translating each sensory pathway toward robot or simulation use; the packaging of the corpus with its citation graph as a self-contained laboratory tool; and the evaluation of AI-assisted literature triage for expanding the database, before closing by mapping the corpus onto the controller's sensory channels.
 
-Chapter~\ref{ch:sns} builds the tools for neuromechanical simulation, with a stated goal: a closed-loop environment in which a synthetic nervous system controls the experimentally characterized robot model and supports the lesion, stimulation, and sensory-deletion experiments that cannot be performed on living subjects. It opens with the prior AnimatLab bipedal walker, whose hand-tuned two-layer CPG produced suspended stepping but whose afferent feedback did not generalize to weight-bearing motion; that outcome set the verification-first approach the tools follow. The tools themselves are the OpenSim-to-MuJoCo conversion of the robot's musculoskeletal model with its synthetic BPA knee, the Simulink SNS library built to prototype reflex topologies, and the cross-platform verification protocol that ties the implementations together. Each tool is demonstrated on the fundamental exercises that establish what it does and does not show: cross-engine reproduction of the contact-driven rhythm core, headless re-runs of the archived AnimatLab reference models, the BPA-actuated walker in MuJoCo, a gait-validation sweep against a locomotion reference library, and a comparison of the production walker variants. The stimulus and deletion experiments complete the demonstrations, and the chapter closes by discussing the five-stage training curriculum, the spiking-neuron mirrors, and the new tools the program leaves other researchers.
